---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import FAQSection from '@components/article/FAQSection.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '@components/article/RelatedArticles.astro';

// Article metadata
const title = 'Remote Team Collaboration Best Practices: The Complete Guide (2025)';
const description = 'Build a high-performing distributed team with proven collaboration strategies. Learn tools, processes, and culture tips for seamless remote teamwork.';
const author = 'FloatJet Team';
const authorUrl = '/about';
const pubDate = new Date('2025-11-24');
const readingTime = 15;
const image = 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1552664730-d307ca884978?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80';
const imageAlt = 'Remote team members collaborating on digital whiteboard';

// Table of contents headings
const headings = [
  {text: 'Introduction', slug: 'introduction', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Building Collaboration Culture', slug: 'culture', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Choosing Your Tool Stack', slug: 'tools', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Documentation as Collaboration', slug: 'documentation', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Sync vs Async Collaboration', slug: 'sync-vs-async', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Project Management for Distributed Teams', slug: 'project-management', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Creative Collaboration Remotely', slug: 'creative', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Code and Design Reviews', slug: 'reviews', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Building Team Cohesion', slug: 'cohesion', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Common Pitfalls', slug: 'pitfalls', depth: 2},
  {text: 'FAQ', slug: 'faq', depth: 2},
];

// FAQ data
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How do I collaborate with team members in very different time zones?',
    answer: '<p>Time zone gaps require intentional strategies:</p><ul><li><strong>Overlap hours:</strong> Identify the 2-4 hours where everyone can meet if truly necessary</li><li><strong>Async-first:</strong> Default to written communication with clear response time expectations</li><li><strong>Handoff docs:</strong> End-of-day summaries that set up the next person for success</li><li><strong>Rotating meeting times:</strong> Share the inconvenience of early/late calls fairly</li><li><strong>Recorded updates:</strong> Use video messages instead of live meetings when possible</li></ul><p>The key is recognizing that most work can be async, reducing the need for real-time overlap.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I know if my team is collaborating effectively?',
    answer: '<p>Signs of healthy collaboration:</p><ul><li>Projects progress without constant meetings or check-ins</li><li>Documentation is current and people reference it</li><li>Team members help each other without being asked</li><li>Decisions are made transparently with clear rationale</li><li>Conflict is addressed directly and constructively</li><li>New team members can onboard from existing docs</li></ul><p>Warning signs: everything requires a meeting, information is hoarded, people work in silos, decisions happen in private channels.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'What tools are essential for remote collaboration?',
    answer: '<p>A minimal effective stack includes:</p><ul><li><strong>Communication:</strong> Slack, Teams, or similar for async messaging</li><li><strong>Video:</strong> Zoom, Meet, or Teams for necessary sync meetings</li><li><strong>Project management:</strong> Notion, ClickUp, Asana, or similar for work tracking</li><li><strong>Documents:</strong> Google Workspace or Microsoft 365 for collaborative editing</li><li><strong>Visual collaboration:</strong> Miro, FigJam, or similar for workshops and brainstorms</li></ul><p>Avoid tool sprawl—better to master fewer tools than have dozens that nobody uses well.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I handle disagreements in remote collaboration?',
    answer: '<p>Remote disagreements require extra care because tone is easily misread:</p><ol><li><strong>Assume good intent:</strong> Written messages often sound harsher than intended</li><li><strong>Move to video:</strong> Complex disagreements benefit from seeing faces and hearing tone</li><li><strong>Focus on interests, not positions:</strong> Understand what each person is trying to achieve</li><li><strong>Document the decision:</strong> Once resolved, write down what was decided and why</li><li><strong>Follow up:</strong> Check in later to ensure the resolution is working</li></ol><p>Don\'t let disagreements fester in async—schedule a call if back-and-forth continues.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I prevent information silos in distributed teams?',
    answer: '<p>Information silos are a common remote work failure mode. Prevent them by:</p><ul><li><strong>Default to public:</strong> Use public channels unless there\'s a specific reason for private</li><li><strong>Document decisions:</strong> Create searchable records of why choices were made</li><li><strong>Regular cross-team updates:</strong> Share what each team is working on</li><li><strong>Rotating participation:</strong> Include different people in meetings and projects</li><li><strong>Centralized knowledge base:</strong> One place everyone knows to look for information</li></ul><p>Silos form when communication is easy within teams but friction exists between them. Reduce that friction.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I onboard new team members remotely?',
    answer: '<p>Remote onboarding requires more structure than in-office:</p><ul><li><strong>Pre-day 1:</strong> Send welcome package, equipment, and account setup instructions</li><li><strong>First week schedule:</strong> Structured calendar with introductions, training sessions, and 1:1s</li><li><strong>Onboarding buddy:</strong> Dedicated person for questions and social connection</li><li><strong>Documentation:</strong> Comprehensive wiki covering processes, tools, and culture</li><li><strong>Quick wins:</strong> Small tasks that let them contribute meaningfully early</li><li><strong>Regular check-ins:</strong> Frequent 1:1s during first 90 days</li></ul><p>Good remote onboarding documentation benefits everyone, not just new hires.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I balance collaboration with deep work time?',
    answer: '<p>Both matter—the key is intentional scheduling:</p><ul><li><strong>Meeting-free blocks:</strong> Designated times (e.g., mornings) with no scheduled collaboration</li><li><strong>Focus modes:</strong> Use tools\' status features to signal unavailability</li><li><strong>Async defaults:</strong> Most collaboration can happen asynchronously</li><li><strong>Calendar blocking:</strong> Protect deep work time like you protect meetings</li><li><strong>Team agreements:</strong> Shared understanding of when real-time response is expected</li></ul><p>The goal isn\'t eliminating collaboration—it\'s making it intentional rather than interrupt-driven.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'What\'s the best way to run remote brainstorming sessions?',
    answer: '<p>Remote brainstorming can actually be more effective than in-person when done right:</p><ol><li><strong>Define the challenge:</strong> Clear problem statement shared in advance</li><li><strong>Individual ideation first:</strong> 5-10 minutes of silent, solo idea generation in a shared space (Miro, FigJam)</li><li><strong>Share and build:</strong> Walk through ideas, comment, combine, and expand</li><li><strong>Voting:</strong> Dot voting or ranking to surface top ideas</li><li><strong>Action planning:</strong> Next steps for promising ideas</li></ol><p>The silent start prevents groupthink and gives introverts equal voice.</p>'
  }
];

// Related articles
const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: 'Best Team Communication Tools',
    excerpt: 'Compare top messaging and collaboration platforms for remote teams.',
    href: '/blog/best-team-communication-tools',
    image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1600880292203-757bb62b4baf?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    imageAlt: 'Team using communication software'
  },
  {
    title: 'Best Project Management Software',
    excerpt: 'Find the perfect project management tool for organizing your distributed team workflows.',
    href: '/tools/best-project-management-software',
    image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1454165804606-c3d57bc86b40?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    imageAlt: 'Modern workspace with project management dashboard'
  },
  {
    title: 'Async Communication for Remote Teams',
    excerpt: 'Master asynchronous communication to reduce meetings and boost productivity.',
    href: '/blog/async-communication-remote-teams',
    image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1522071820081-009f0129c71c?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    imageAlt: 'Remote team collaborating through digital tools'
  }
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Communication"
    categorySlug="guides/communication"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">Collaboration Without Co-location</h2>
  <p>
    Remote work has proven that proximity isn't required for productivity. Teams across time zones and continents
    build successful products every day. But effective remote collaboration doesn't happen automatically—it requires
    intentional design of processes, tools, and culture.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The best distributed teams don't try to replicate the office remotely.</strong> They embrace the unique
    advantages of remote work: deep focus time, async communication, written documentation, and global talent access.
    They also acknowledge the challenges and address them directly.
  </p>
  <p>
    This guide covers everything from tool selection to culture building, with practical strategies you can
    implement immediately.
  </p>

  <!-- Building Culture -->
  <h2 id="culture">Building a Collaboration Culture</h2>
  <p>
    Tools don't create collaboration—culture does. The best tools fail without norms around how to use them.
  </p>

  <h3>Establish Explicit Norms</h3>
  <p>
    In an office, culture is absorbed through observation. Remote teams must make culture explicit:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Communication expectations:</strong> When to use which channel, expected response times</li>
    <li><strong>Meeting culture:</strong> Camera on/off norms, agenda requirements, decision documentation</li>
    <li><strong>Working hours:</strong> Core hours, flexibility boundaries, timezone considerations</li>
    <li><strong>Feedback culture:</strong> How to give and receive feedback</li>
    <li><strong>Decision-making:</strong> Who can decide what, and how decisions are communicated</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Default to Transparency</h3>
  <p>
    Information hoarding kills remote collaboration. Adopt a transparency-first mindset:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Use public channels unless there's a specific reason for private</li>
    <li>Document decisions and their rationale</li>
    <li>Share context generously—others can't overhear hallway conversations</li>
    <li>Make calendars visible so teammates understand availability</li>
    <li>Surface blockers and challenges, not just wins</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Trust by Default</h3>
  <p>
    Remote collaboration requires trusting teammates to manage their own work. Micromanagement doesn't scale
    and signals lack of trust. Instead:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Focus on outcomes, not hours worked</li>
    <li>Give context and goals, not step-by-step instructions</li>
    <li>Allow flexibility in how and when work gets done</li>
    <li>Address issues directly when they arise, rather than adding surveillance</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Invest in Relationships</h3>
  <p>
    Collaboration flows easier between people who know and trust each other. Remote teams must intentionally
    create opportunities for connection:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Virtual coffee chats and social channels</li>
    <li>Team rituals (Friday celebrations, Monday kickoffs)</li>
    <li>In-person gatherings when possible</li>
    <li>Space for non-work conversation in meetings</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Tool Stack -->
  <h2 id="tools">Choosing the Right Tool Stack</h2>
  <p>
    Your tool stack is the foundation of remote collaboration. Choose deliberately, implement thoughtfully,
    and resist tool sprawl.
  </p>

  <h3>The Essential Categories</h3>

  <h4>Communication Hub</h4>
  <p>
    Your primary space for team conversation. This is where most async collaboration happens.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Slack:</strong> Feature-rich, extensive integrations, industry standard</li>
    <li><strong>Microsoft Teams:</strong> Best for Microsoft 365 organizations</li>
    <li><strong>Discord:</strong> Great for smaller teams, strong community features</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    See our <a href="/blog/best-team-communication-tools">complete guide to team communication tools</a> for detailed
    comparisons.
  </p>

  <h4>Project Management</h4>
  <p>
    The source of truth for what work exists, who owns it, and what stage it's in.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Notion:</strong> Flexible workspace combining docs, databases, and project tracking</li>
    <li><strong>ClickUp:</strong> Feature-rich PM with docs, goals, and time tracking built in</li>
    <li><strong>Asana:</strong> Clean task management with excellent team features</li>
    <li><strong>Linear:</strong> Modern issue tracking, popular with engineering teams</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Check our <a href="/tools/best-project-management-software">project management software comparison</a> for detailed
    reviews.
  </p>

  <h4>Document Collaboration</h4>
  <p>
    Real-time co-editing of documents, spreadsheets, and presentations.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Google Workspace:</strong> Excellent real-time collaboration, web-native</li>
    <li><strong>Microsoft 365:</strong> Familiar interfaces, strong enterprise features</li>
    <li><strong>Notion:</strong> Great for knowledge bases and team wikis</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Video Conferencing</h4>
  <p>
    For when real-time face-to-face is needed.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zoom:</strong> Most feature-rich, best for large meetings</li>
    <li><strong>Google Meet:</strong> Simple, integrated with Google Workspace</li>
    <li><strong>Microsoft Teams:</strong> Built into the Teams ecosystem</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Visual Collaboration</h4>
  <p>
    For workshops, brainstorms, and anything that benefits from visual thinking.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Miro:</strong> Comprehensive whiteboarding with templates for everything</li>
    <li><strong>FigJam:</strong> Simpler interface, great for design teams</li>
    <li><strong>Excalidraw:</strong> Free, lightweight sketching tool</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Avoiding Tool Sprawl</h3>
  <p>
    Every new tool adds cognitive load and fragments information. Before adding a tool:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Can existing tools do this job adequately?</li>
    <li>Who will maintain and champion the tool?</li>
    <li>How will it integrate with existing workflows?</li>
    <li>What problem does it solve that justifies the overhead?</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Documentation -->
  <h2 id="documentation">Documentation as Collaboration</h2>
  <p>
    In distributed teams, documentation isn't just record-keeping—it's how knowledge spreads and collaboration scales.
  </p>

  <h3>Write for Discoverability</h3>
  <p>
    Documentation is useless if people can't find it. Structure for search:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Consistent naming conventions</li>
    <li>Clear folder/page hierarchy</li>
    <li>Liberal use of tags and cross-links</li>
    <li>Searchable titles that match how people ask questions</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Write for Future Readers</h3>
  <p>
    Assume readers lack your context. Include:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The "why":</strong> Context and reasoning, not just conclusions</li>
    <li><strong>Definitions:</strong> Acronyms and jargon explained</li>
    <li><strong>Links:</strong> References to related documents</li>
    <li><strong>Dates:</strong> When the document was written and last updated</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Keep Documentation Alive</h3>
  <p>
    Outdated documentation is worse than no documentation. Build maintenance into workflows:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Assign owners to key documents</li>
    <li>Schedule regular reviews</li>
    <li>Make updating docs part of completing work</li>
    <li>Archive rather than delete outdated content</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Document Decisions</h3>
  <p>
    Every significant decision should be documented with:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>What was decided</li>
    <li>Why (context, options considered, reasoning)</li>
    <li>Who was involved</li>
    <li>When it was made</li>
    <li>Any caveats or conditions</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This prevents "why did we do this?" conversations months later.
  </p>

  <!-- Sync vs Async -->
  <h2 id="sync-vs-async">Synchronous vs Asynchronous Collaboration</h2>
  <p>
    Understanding when to go sync vs async is crucial for effective remote collaboration.
    Learn more in our detailed <a href="/blog/async-communication-remote-teams">async communication guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Default to Async</h3>
  <p>
    Most collaboration can happen asynchronously, which:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Respects different time zones and working hours</li>
    <li>Creates documentation automatically</li>
    <li>Allows for deeper thinking before responding</li>
    <li>Protects focus time</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Use Sync Strategically</h3>
  <p>
    Real-time collaboration is valuable for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Relationship building and team bonding</li>
    <li>Sensitive or emotional conversations</li>
    <li>Rapid iteration when multiple decisions depend on each other</li>
    <li>Complex problems benefiting from real-time back-and-forth</li>
    <li>Emergencies requiring immediate coordination</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Hybrid Patterns</h3>
  <p>
    Many processes work best with a mix:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Async prep → Sync meeting → Async follow-up:</strong> Share materials in advance, discuss key points
      live, document outcomes after
    </li>
    <li><strong>Async brainstorm → Sync synthesis:</strong> Generate ideas individually, then come together to discuss
      top contenders
    </li>
    <li><strong>Sync kickoff → Async work → Sync review:</strong> Align at start and end, independent work in between
    </li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Project Management -->
  <h2 id="project-management">Project Management for Distributed Teams</h2>
  <p>
    Effective project management becomes even more critical when teams aren't co-located.
  </p>

  <h3>Single Source of Truth</h3>
  <p>
    Everyone should know where to look for project status. This means:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>One project management tool, consistently used</li>
    <li>Updates happen in the tool, not just in meetings</li>
    <li>Decisions and context recorded on relevant tasks</li>
    <li>Clear ownership for every work item</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Visibility Without Micromanagement</h3>
  <p>
    Remote teams need visibility into work progress, but this shouldn't feel like surveillance:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Focus on outcomes, not activity metrics</li>
    <li>Let people update status on their own schedule</li>
    <li>Use automated updates where possible</li>
    <li>Trust people to flag blockers</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Async Standups</h3>
  <p>
    Daily standup meetings often waste time. Try async alternatives:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Written updates in a dedicated channel</li>
    <li>Brief video check-ins recorded at convenient times</li>
    <li>Automated bots that prompt for updates</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Save sync time for discussing blockers, not reciting status.
  </p>

  <!-- Creative Collaboration -->
  <h2 id="creative">Creative Collaboration Remotely</h2>
  <p>
    Creative work can thrive remotely with the right approaches.
  </p>

  <h3>Design Collaboration</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Figma:</strong> Real-time collaborative design with built-in commenting</li>
    <li><strong>FigJam:</strong> Whiteboarding for design workshops</li>
    <li><strong>Loom:</strong> Walk through designs with video commentary</li>
    <li><strong>Markup tools:</strong> Annotate designs for async feedback</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Writing Collaboration</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Google Docs:</strong> Real-time co-editing with suggesting mode</li>
    <li><strong>Notion:</strong> Collaborative editing with comments and mentions</li>
    <li><strong>Hemingway Editor:</strong> Shared link for style feedback</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Brainstorming Remotely</h3>
  <p>
    Remote brainstorming can be more effective than in-person when structured well:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Silent generation:</strong> Everyone adds ideas independently (prevents groupthink)</li>
    <li><strong>Sharing:</strong> Walk through ideas without judgment</li>
    <li><strong>Building:</strong> Combine and extend promising ideas</li>
    <li><strong>Voting:</strong> Prioritize top ideas democratically</li>
  </ol>

  <!-- Reviews -->
  <h2 id="reviews">Code and Design Reviews</h2>
  <p>
    Reviews are critical collaboration points for quality and knowledge sharing.
  </p>

  <h3>Code Review Best Practices</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Small, focused PRs:</strong> Easier to review thoroughly</li>
    <li><strong>Clear descriptions:</strong> Context, approach, testing done</li>
    <li><strong>Async by default:</strong> Give reviewers time to think</li>
    <li><strong>Timely turnaround:</strong> Don't let PRs languish</li>
    <li><strong>Constructive tone:</strong> Explain reasoning, not just opinions</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Design Review Best Practices</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Context first:</strong> Explain the problem being solved</li>
    <li><strong>Multiple options:</strong> Show alternatives considered</li>
    <li><strong>Specific feedback requested:</strong> What input do you need?</li>
    <li><strong>Async comments:</strong> Let people digest before discussing</li>
    <li><strong>Decision documentation:</strong> Record final choices and reasoning</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Team Cohesion -->
  <h2 id="cohesion">Building Team Cohesion Virtually</h2>
  <p>
    Collaboration is easier when people know and trust each other. Remote teams must intentionally create
    connection opportunities.
  </p>

  <h3>Virtual Social Activities</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Coffee chats:</strong> Random pairings for casual conversation</li>
    <li><strong>Team games:</strong> Trivia, escape rooms, Jackbox games</li>
    <li><strong>Show and tell:</strong> Share hobbies, travels, pets</li>
    <li><strong>Celebrations:</strong> Acknowledge wins, birthdays, milestones</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Social Channels</h3>
  <p>
    Create spaces for non-work conversation:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>#random or #watercooler for general chat</li>
    <li>Interest-based channels (pets, cooking, gaming)</li>
    <li>Local channels for people in similar time zones</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>In-Person Gatherings</h3>
  <p>
    If budget allows, periodic in-person time accelerates relationship building:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Annual or semi-annual team retreats</li>
    <li>Regional meetups for nearby team members</li>
    <li>Conference attendance together</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Pitfalls -->
  <h2 id="pitfalls">Common Collaboration Pitfalls</h2>

  <h3>1. Over-Meeting</h3>
  <p>
    Replacing office hallway conversations with Zoom calls leads to meeting overload.
    <strong>Fix:</strong> Default to async, use meetings intentionally.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Information Silos</h3>
  <p>
    Work happens in DMs and private channels, leaving others without context.
    <strong>Fix:</strong> Public by default, document decisions.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Async Anxiety</h3>
  <p>
    People stress about response times without clear expectations.
    <strong>Fix:</strong> Explicit norms about response times and urgency indicators.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Tool Chaos</h3>
  <p>
    Too many tools, inconsistently used, with fragmented information.
    <strong>Fix:</strong> Consolidate tools, establish clear purposes for each.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Proximity Bias</h3>
  <p>
    In hybrid settings, remote workers get excluded from decisions made in-office.
    <strong>Fix:</strong> Remote-first processes even when some people are co-located.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Burning Out</h3>
  <p>
    Without clear boundaries, work expands to fill all available time.
    <strong>Fix:</strong> Respect working hours, model healthy boundaries.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <h2>Conclusion: Collaboration as Competitive Advantage</h2>
  <p>
    Remote collaboration isn't about replicating office dynamics online—it's about building something better.
    Distributed teams that master collaboration gain access to global talent, create comprehensive documentation,
    respect focus time, and build more inclusive workplaces.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Start with culture and norms.</strong> Tools are important, but they're only as effective as the
    agreements around how to use them. Establish explicit expectations for communication, decision-making,
    and documentation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Default to async and transparency.</strong> Real-time collaboration has its place, but async
    should be the default. Public channels and documented decisions prevent information silos and scale
    knowledge sharing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Invest in relationships.</strong> Collaboration flows easier between people who trust each other.
    Create intentional spaces for connection beyond work tasks.
  </p>
  <p>
    The teams that figure out remote collaboration don't just survive distributed work—they thrive because of it.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
